{
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "field": "CHEMISTRY",
      "title": "The chemistry of the red and the black",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Ajrakh colours come from indigo, madder and iron acetate.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "feaa25b3-b79c-59ce-9c6b-1a841c7e12ed"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "4ce205e3-653f-51db-9e49-14fb3b3f2e14"
          ],
          "cites": [
            4
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Kut is made from jaggery or molasses in which scrap iron such as horseshoes and gram flour have been fermented for ten to fifteen days; the iron-water is mixed with tree gum.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "c407fde1-3c40-5c81-8a5e-320317aacf9d"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "On a Met Indian block-printed fragment, iron acetate made from fermented scrap iron, together with myrobalan tannin, formed a corrosive dye that caused fibre loss.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "6401aedc-bb18-5c46-914b-c614fdec277e"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "8a32aea5-44f7-5a50-942b-97534f0e5d8e"
          ],
          "cites": [
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Indigo can be natural (Indigofera tinctoria) or synthetic 'German' indigo; natural indigo is fermented with lime, dates and soda; synthetic indigo often has 'Hydros' added.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "446933ae-9d7a-53ab-b2f2-da71f429fbef"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Indigo is not soluble in water whereas leucoindigo is; indigo is transformed to leucoindigo by indigo-reducing bacteria.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "a5761723-d8b3-5547-aa5c-919a865593de"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "48309f78-3f0e-511e-a874-703135c913a6"
          ],
          "cites": [
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Indigo is reduced to soluble 'indigo white' for dyeing and fixed to the fibre by air oxidation.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "84e0cfb0-4a8a-5e08-8475-6cfca77831d5"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "84ad0382-a600-5df1-8b10-3220a1e6df1a"
          ],
          "cites": [
            7
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At first bright green, the cloth rapidly becomes blue as oxygen meets the dye on the fibres.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "90d52dfd-901a-5287-b34a-16498a1a1912"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Dyed textiles are briefly exposed to air to oxidise leucoindigo back to indigo.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "00cedcde-83f0-541b-91ab-27b13ffb72b5"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "48309f78-3f0e-511e-a874-703135c913a6"
          ],
          "cites": [
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Alizarin crystals or steeped madder roots are heated in a copper pot; dyeing the purest reds continues for up to four hours.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "cd8140e6-98fc-5882-b261-766e143dd8c2"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Indian madder roots (Rubia cordifolia), locally majeeta, link to the alum mordant on cotton to develop red where pa and gach were printed.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "542cae98-9a85-5410-a375-69334290d130"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Alizarin, first synthesised in 1868 by Graebe and Liebermann, superseded madder and also requires alum to attach colour.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "0899069c-80aa-5b60-bfb9-79eee94a39fe"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Alizarin molecules can form six-member chelate rings with aluminium ions; it is also likely that the large complexes are physically trapped within the fibre.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "c4727f5f-13c0-5fb0-8f18-d579a8d92ace"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "9187df8b-62e3-510a-ba5d-827ec098c84c"
          ],
          "cites": [
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Alum is used as a mordant to fasten the colours.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "1dcf0fe9-1f1c-518e-868b-5e54bcd44a2e"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Alum is suspended in a sticky paste of boiled powder from roasted and ground tamarind seeds (kachika).",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "924ebc33-e949-5655-95a4-538d13f8fca7"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The final wash is usually done early in the morning when water is cool, as warm afternoon water may dissolve some of the alum.",
          "level": "PROVISIONAL",
          "claimIds": [
            "aef422e3-a33e-5e74-96f4-3268621b710d"
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8"
          ],
          "cites": [
            1
          ]
        }
      ],
